            <ArticleTitle>A novel role for renal epithelial cells and the medullary sodium gradient in the local immune response.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1308-1311</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0085-2538(17)30732-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.kint.2017.10.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The extreme hypertonicity of the renal medulla plays a central role in regulating volume status. A recent publication in Cell has identified a novel role for the high sodium environment and the local epithelial cells in the recruitment of mononuclear phagocytes, potentially contributing to the defense against ascending urinary tract infection.</AbstractText>
